The problem asks us to find the number of connected simple undirected graphs with $N$ vertices and $M$ edges such that the number of vertices at an even distance from vertex 1 is equal to the number of vertices at an odd distance from vertex 1. We need to output this count modulo $P$ for all $M$ from $N-1$ to $\frac{N(N-1)}{2}$.
$N$ is an even integer, $2 \le N \le 30$. $P$ is a prime, $10^8 \le P \le 10^9$.

Let $d(v)$ be the shortest distance from vertex 1 to vertex $v$ in the graph.
The condition is $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$.
Since there are $N$ vertices in total, this means exactly $N/2$ vertices have even distance and $N/2$ vertices have odd distance.
Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So vertex 1 is always counted in the even set. Thus, we need exactly $N/2 - 1$ other vertices to have even distance, and $N/2$ vertices to have odd distance.

Let's denote the set of vertices with even distance as $V_{even}$ and odd distance as $V_{odd}$.
We require $|V_{even}| = |V_{odd}| = N/2$.
Also, $1 \in V_{even}$.

